 El mismo análisis de *Biomédica* sitúa la obesidad en adultos en 18,7% en 2015, y el ICBF usa esa misma cifra (uno de cada cinco es obeso (18,7%)). Los 22,4% de mujeres son por tanto un dato específico de ellas, no un promedio nacional.\n- **Actividad física:** Aproximadamente la mitad de los adultos colombianos realiza 150 minutos semanales de actividad física moderada o 75 minutos semanales de actividad vigorosa o fuerte, como lo recomienda la OMS. La prensa nacional reportó además que factores como el sedentarismo, la mala alimentación y la falta de educación en nutrición sana han aumentado las cifras de obesidad, pero no encontré desglose de actividad física por sexo en estos resultados.\n\nAdvertencia: una ficha del ICBF indica un exceso de peso de 6,3% en 2015, que es incompatible con el 56,4% de la misma institución y probablemente es un error de transcripción o se refiere a otro indicador. Conviene verificar la cifra directamente en el informe oficial de la ENSIN 2015 del Ministerio de Salud antes de citarla."],"durationSeconds":6.848614249999999,"searchCount":1},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01RC2RyGHaunPCFTP788WjmD","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NYbBD37brAyAd4tPqE2ewH","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"cleavers Galium aparine red clover lymphatic evidence diuretic study\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Cleavers's therapeutic uses\",\"url\":\"https://www.ebsco.com/research-starters/complementary-and-alternative-medicine/cleaverss-therapeutic-uses\"},{\"title\":\"Evidence supporting the use of: Clivers\",\"url\":\"https://vitabase.com/relationships/relationship-lymphatics-and-clivers\"},{\"title\":\"Parts Used & Where Grown\",\"url\":\"https://uofmhealth.org/health-library/hn-2070002\"},{\"title\":\"christopherhobbs.com\",\"url\":\"https://christopherhobbs.com/?p=3689\"},{\"title\":\"Cleavers: The Stage 4 Clinger That’s Actually Good for You\",\"url\":\"https://thebuffaloherbalist.substack.com/p/cleavers-the-stage-4-clinger-thats\"},{\"title\":\"Monograph #026\",\"url\":\"https://meridian-medica.pages.dev/cleavers\"},{\"title\":\"hn 2070002\",\"url\":\"https://www.peacehealth.org/medical-topics/id/hn-2070002\"},{\"title\":\"Evidence supporting the use of: Cleavers\",\"url\":\"https://vitabase.com/relationships/relationship-lymph-nodes-or-glands-swollen-and-cleavers-2\"},{\"title\":\"143210 benefits cleavers herb\",\"url\":\"https://www.livestrong.com/article/143210-benefits-cleavers-herb\"},{\"title\":\"Cleavers (Galium aparine)\",\"url\":\"https://www.herbrally.com/monographs/cleavers\"}]\n\nThe search found no human clinical trial testing cleavers (Galium aparine) with red clover for lymphatic or diuretic effects. Most sources are traditional-use summaries, and the research they cite is mostly in vitro or animal work.\n\n- **Clinical evidence is thin:** One source says few clinical trials have been conducted on cleavers, though preliminary studies suggest its efficacy as a mild diuretic. Another says while some in vitro and animal studies indicate possible anti-inflammatory and diuretic effects of cleavers, high-quality human trials are lacking.\n- **Lymphatic claims rest on tradition:** Vitabase rates the lymphatic use current evidence is anecdotal and based on historical usage rather than rigorous clinical trials.\n- **Diuretic use is also traditional:** The University of Michigan material, as reproduced by PeacHealth, notes that cleavers is one of numerous plants considered in ancient times to act as a diuretic.\n- **Red clover:** I found no study of red clover combined with cleavers. The only pairing I saw was in a commercial monograph (Meridian Medica) that describes the two as synergize-ing on paper, which is a traditional-practice claim rather than trial data. That same monograph says the highest-value research gap includes the absence of clinical comparisons for lymphatic or diuretic outcomes.\n- **Lower-quality sources:** Herbrally cites a 2016 breast cancer cell study (Atmaca) and a single UTI case report, but neither addresses lymphatic function or diuresis in humans. EBSCO also warns that people taking lithium should only use cleavers under a physician's supervision because of dehydration risk.\n\nBottom line: the lymphatic and diuretic uses are mainly traditional, supported by limited in vitro and animal work, and not confirmed in human trials. No direct evidence for the red clover combination turned up. A PubMed search on Galium aparine diuretic and Trifolium pratense lymphatic trials would be the next step.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3373edb3-ea35-4c09-93cb-41b8e7cea5f5","uuid":"10d41cca-aeae-446b-ac13-5760373dc6d4","timestamp":"2026-11-28T11:12:25.483Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"cleavers Galium aparine red clover lymphatic evidence diuretic study","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_012BgUSCRGspSCUC6z4KXU7J","content":[{"title":"Cleavers's therapeutic uses","url":"https://www.ebsco.com/research-starters/complementary-and-alternative-