 study of red clover combined with cleavers. The only pairing I saw was in a commercial monograph (Meridian Medica) that describes the two as synergize-ing on paper, which is a traditional-practice claim rather than trial data. That same monograph says the highest-value research gap includes the absence of clinical comparisons for lymphatic or diuretic outcomes.\n- **Lower-quality sources:** Herbrally cites a 2016 breast cancer cell study (Atmaca) and a single UTI case report, but neither addresses lymphatic function or diuresis in humans. EBSCO also warns that people taking lithium should only use cleavers under a physician's supervision because of dehydration risk.\n\nBottom line: the lymphatic and diuretic uses are mainly traditional, supported by limited in vitro and animal work, and not confirmed in human trials. No direct evidence for the red clover combination turned up. A PubMed search on Galium aparine diuretic and Trifolium pratense lymphatic trials would be the next step."],"durationSeconds":6.7578530410000095,"searchCount":1},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01NYbBD37brAyAd4tPqE2ewH","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
